---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ndsuPhoto from "@/assets/images/profile/NDSUparents.jpg";
import coachingPhoto from "@/assets/images/profile/KilosandKegs.jpg";
import bearPhoto from "@/assets/images/profile/Bear.jpg";

const metadata = {
  title: "About Austin Widmer",
  description:
    "A background in mechanical and aerospace engineering, astrodynamics research, and weightlifting coaching.",
};
---

<BaseLayout {metadata}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:py-20 lg:px-8">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase text-primary">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">
        Austin Widmer
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        I am an engineer and weightlifting coach from Fargo, North Dakota, now studying aerospace engineering at Texas A&amp;M University. 
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg mt-12 max-w-none dark:prose-invert">
      <p>
        In 2022, I earned a bachelor's degree in mechanical engineering from North Dakota
        State University. I spent a year designing agricultural machinery at Crary Industries, 
        in West Fargo, ND, before deciding to pursue a graduate degree.
        I then moved to Texas to pursue a master's degree in aerospace engineering at Texas A&amp;M University.
        During my graduate studies, I focused on optimal control theory and its applications to aerospace systems, 
        studying under Dr. John L. Junkins. I completed my master's of science in 2026, and have choosen 
        to continue working towards a doctoral degree in aerospace engineering.
      </p>
      <p>
        My engineering experience has spanned a handful of different fields. I currently work at 
        the Aerospace Corporation as a graduate student intern, where I contribute to the development 
        of optimization and astrodynamics software tools for aerospace applications. My academic research 
        focuses on many of the same techniques, leveraging optimal control theory and advanced computational methods 
        to solve trajectory optimization problems in astrodynamics contexts.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my mechanical engineering career, I gained hands-on experience in designing and analyzing large-scale 
        machinery and hydraulic systems. Mechanical design, material selection &amp; analysis, and design for manufacturing 
        were central to my work, providing a very practical foundation for my current work in aerospace engineering.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside of engineering and academia, I have always pursued athletic endeavors. In high school, 
        I competed in the track &amp; field throwing events, which lead me to develop an interest in 
        strength training and the sport of (olympic-style) weightlifting. During my undergraduate studies, 
        I began competing in weightlifting, and soon after began coaching other athletes in the sport. 
        When I moved to Texas A&amp;M University, I got involved with the Texas A&amp;M University weightlifting club,
        and soon after I filled the role of head coach for the club. Since then we've been able to grow the program from 
        less than 10 athletes to more than 30, and have been able to attend the University National Championships several times. 
        You can learn more about the club at: <a href="https://tamuweightlifting.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"> https://tamuweightlifting.com</a>.
      </p>
      <!-- <p>
        I also collaborated with developer David Lindberg on a web-based,
        data-driven weightlifting coaching application:
        <a href="https://app.widmertraining.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Widmer Training</a
        >.
      </p> -->
      <p>
        When I'm not engaged in engineering, coaching, or training, I enjoy working on a handful of hobbies, including 3D printing, 
        custom apparel design, carpentry, CNC projects, and spending time outdoors with my dog, Bear.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={ndsuPhoto}
          alt="Austin with his parents at North Dakota State University"
          class="aspect-[4/3] w-full object-cover"
          widths={[480, 720]}
          sizes="(min-width: 640px) 45vw, 90vw"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          North Dakota State University
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={coachingPhoto}
          alt="Texas A&M weightlifting team"
          class="aspect-[4/3] w-full object-cover"
          widths={[480, 720]}
          sizes="(min-width: 640px) 45vw, 90vw"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          Aggie weightlifting
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="sm:col-span-2 sm:max-w-xl">
        <Image
          src={bearPhoto}
          alt="Bear, Austin's dog"
          class="aspect-[16/9] w-full object-cover"
          widths={[480, 720, 960]}
          sizes="(min-width: 640px) 70vw, 90vw"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Bear</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>